<ToolLayout title="Color Picker & Converter: HEX, RGB, HSL | FreeOnlineToolkit" description="Color Picker & Converter: HEX, RGB, HSL. Free online calculator included." breadcrumbItems={breadcrumbItems} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <fragment slot="heading"><h1>Color Picker & Converter: HEX, RGB, HSL</h1></fragment>

  <div class="key-takeaways" style="background:#eff6ff;border:1px solid #93c5fd;border-radius:8px;padding:1.25rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0;color:#1e40af;">Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul style="margin-bottom:0;">
      <li>HEX (#RRGGBB), RGB (rgb(r,g,b)), and HSL (hsl(h,s%,l%)) all represent the same colour space - just in different notations optimised for different use cases.</li>
      <li>HEX codes are the standard in web design and CSS - they are compact (7 characters) and universally supported across browsers and design tools.</li>
      <li>HSL (Hue, Saturation, Lightness) is the most intuitive format for creating colour palettes - adjust lightness for shades and tints while keeping the hue constant.</li>
      <li>The CSS Color Level 4 specification added support for modern formats like oklch() and oklab() for perceptually uniform colour manipulation.</li>
      <li>Accessibility matters: WCAG 2.1 requires a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text against their background.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

    <p>Colour is one of the most powerful tools in design, yet working with colours programmatically requires understanding how computers represent what our eyes see. The three most common colour formats - HEX, RGB, and HSL - all describe the same underlying reality (the sRGB colour space) but through different lenses. <strong>HEX</strong> is a 6-digit hexadecimal number where pairs represent red, green, and blue intensity (00-FF, or 0-255 in decimal). <strong>RGB</strong> uses three decimal numbers in the same 0-255 range. <strong>HSL</strong> decomposes colour into hue (the colour wheel angle, 0-360 degrees), saturation (how vivid, 0-100%), and lightness (how bright, 0-100%).</p>

    <p>Each format excels in different contexts. HEX codes dominate in <strong>web design</strong> because they are compact, easy to copy-paste, and supported everywhere from CSS to design tools like Figma and Adobe XD. The shorthand notation (#FFF for #FFFFFF, #636 for #636363) makes them even more concise. RGB is natural for <strong>programmatic manipulation</strong> - if you want to increase the red channel, you simply increment the first value. Modern CSS supports both notations interchangeably.</p>

    <p><strong>HSL</strong> is the most human-friendly format for designers because it aligns with how we think about colour. Want a lighter version of your brand blue? Increase the lightness value while keeping hue and saturation constant. Want a muted variant? Reduce saturation. This makes HSL ideal for generating <strong>colour palettes</strong> - a single hue can produce dozens of coordinated shades, tints, and tones by systematically varying S and L. Many design systems (Tailwind CSS, Material Design) define their colour scales using this principle.</p>

    <p><strong>Colour accessibility</strong> is an increasingly important consideration. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG 2.1) specify minimum contrast ratios between text and background colours to ensure readability for people with visual impairments. Normal text requires a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1, while large text (18pt+) requires 3:1. When choosing colours for user interfaces, always verify contrast ratios - tools like this converter help you experiment with different lightness values to find accessible combinations that still look great.</p>

  <fragment slot="intro">
    <p class="tool-intro">Use the free calculator below to get instant results. Read on for a full guide to the topic, key formulas, and expert tips.</p>
  </fragment>

